# SRG-APP-000439-WSR-000192  -  CAT II
# The web server must use HTTP/2, at a minimum.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000439-WSR-000192
group:           V-264362
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-002418
control_rev5:    SC-8

contribution:    Splunk Web's own HTTP server implements HTTP/1.x only, so the
                 application's whole contribution is to not withhold HTTP/1.1 from clients
                 and to operate correctly behind an HTTP/2 terminator through
                 tools.proxy.on and tools.proxy.base.

governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] forceHttp10 not_equals always
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] forceHttp10 https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        A reverse proxy or load balancer terminating HTTP/2 in front of Splunk
                 Web, since the product's web server does not implement HTTP/2 and no
                 configuration setting selects it
external:        The protocol version that terminator uses on its upstream connection to
                 Splunk Web, which is the terminator's own setting
external:        The requirement that browsers support HTTP/2 only in HTTPS mode, which the
                 fix text notes and which the TLS terminator carries

citation:        SRG-APP-000439-WSR-000192 check and fix text; web.conf.spec:1016-1033,
                 1129-1142
finding:         web.conf [settings] forceHttp10 is set to always, so Splunk Web withholds
                 HTTP/1.1 features from every client and sits further below the required
                 HTTP version than the product requires.
